<spell-page "getting started" notebook>
	<header level="1">
		Getting started
		<header>Install the package, load the components, and use them from any framework.</>
	</>

	<!--
		GETTING STARTED:  the old Astro page (`getting-started.mdx`, deleted in P7:  install, the contract,
		frameworks, styling) plus Fomantic's `introduction/getting-started.html.eco` where it fits our install
		(General Usage:  descriptive class names, class order;  Installation;  Updating).  Fomantic's gulp build,
		semantic.json, CDN and CSS / LESS distributions have no counterpart:  one line says so.
	-->
	<markdown>
		How to put Spell UI on a page, and the three rules every component follows so it works in any
		framework. Something unclear or broken? [Open an issue](https://github.com/spell-app/spell-ui/issues/new).
	</>

	<section label="General Usage">
		<section label="Descriptive Attributes" sticky collapsible dividing>
			<example "Descriptive Attributes" description="Fomantic's base concept is descriptive class names on HTML nodes; Spell UI's is descriptive ATTRIBUTES on `ui-*` tags. The tag's `ui-` prefix does the namespacing Fomantic's `ui` class did.">
				<button color="primary">Button</button>
			</example>
			<example "Descriptive Attributes" description="Combine variations to get several effects on the same component:  `size` and `primary`.">
				<button size="huge" color="primary">Button</button>
			</example>
		</>

		<section label="Attribute Order" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Attribute Order" description="Fomantic needs some class names in a strict order (`ui left spaced right floated image`, never `ui left right image floated spaced`). Attributes have no such rule: each names its own side, so any order renders the same.">
				<segment clearing appearance="basic">
					<image floated="right" spacing="left" size="tiny" src="/components/ui/images/wireframe/square-image.png" alt=""/>
				</segment>
				<segment clearing appearance="basic">
					<image spacing="left" size="tiny" floated="right" src="/components/ui/images/wireframe/square-image.png" alt=""/>
				</segment>
			</example>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Installation">
		<message state="info" heading="Under the tag next until the first release">Until Spell's first release, planned for December 1, 2026, <text code>spell-ui</> is installed as <text code>spell-ui@next</>; from then on, plain <text code>spell-ui</>.</>
		<markdown>
			Requirements: a current browser. Spell UI ASSUMES anchor positioning, popover, `<dialog>`,
			container style queries and form-associated custom elements, with no polyfills.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```bash
			npm install spell-ui@next solid-js@2.0.0-rc.17 @solidjs/web@2.0.0-rc.17
			```
		</>
		<markdown>
			Solid 2 is a peer: your app and Spell UI share one copy. Then load the components, in one of three
			ways:

			- **Everything**: `import "spell-ui"` registers every `ui-*` element.
			- **One family at a time**: each family is its own entry, `import "spell-ui/ui-button"`, so a
			  page loads only what it imports.
			- **On demand**: wrap the page (or an app) in `<ui-root>` and import only `spell-ui/ui-root`.
			  Each `ui-*` tag inside loads its family the first time it appears. This site works that way.

			With the whole kit installed instead (`npm install spell@next`), the same entries are
			`spell/ui`, `spell/ui/ui-button` and `spell/ui/ui-root`. A page loads one or the other, never both.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			import "spell-ui/ui-root" // the root;  it loads every other family on first use
			```
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<ui-root icons="fa7-brands" display="immediately">
			  <ui-button color="primary">Save</ui-button>
			</ui-root>
			```
		</>
		<markdown>
			For pages with no JavaScript at all, `ui.css` (`src/styles/ui.css`, a build entry) is the whole page
			foundation as one stylesheet: tokens, utilities, typography and native markup. Add
			`class="ui-typography"` to `<body>` for Fomantic's page typography.
		</>
		<message size="small" class="site-not-ported">Not ported: Fomantic's <text code>gulp</> build, <text code>semantic.json</>, CDN links and separate CSS / LESS / SASS distributions -- one package holds the elements, their CSS and the token sheets, and themes are CSS files you add, not a build.</>
	</>

	<section label="The Contract">
		<markdown>
			Every component follows the same three rules, which is what makes it work in every framework:

			- **Properties for data.** Rich data (`options`, `rows`) is a JS property, a real accessor on the
			  class, so frameworks find it with `key in el`. First paint never needs one: server rendering
			  drops object properties.
			- **Attributes for primitives.** Strings, numbers and booleans are reflected attributes. Booleans
			  follow HTML: presence, `""`, `"true"` or `"yes"` mean true; `"false"` and `"no"` mean false (see
			  [Grammar](/grammar)).
			- **`ui-*` events.** Every event is a `CustomEvent` with `bubbles: true, composed: true` and a
			  lowercase kebab name (`ui-changed`, `ui-opening`). `detail` carries the computed state, e.g.
			  `{ value }`, plus `originalEvent`.
		</>

		<section label="Plain HTML" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<markdown>
				No framework:  attributes in markup, the `options` property and a `ui-changed` listener in script.

				```html
				<ui-dropdown id="contract-dropdown" selection placeholder="Fruit" aria-label="Fruit"></ui-dropdown>
				<ui-label id="contract-value" pointing="left" appearance="basic">nothing chosen</ui-label>
				<script type="module">
				  const dropdown = document.querySelector("#contract-dropdown")
				  dropdown.options = [
				    { value: "apple", text: "Apple" },
				    { value: "pear", text: "Pear" }
				  ]
				  dropdown.addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => {
				    document.querySelector("#contract-value").textContent = event.detail.value
				  })
				</script>
				```
			</>
		</>
		<markdown>
			The script above sets `options` before `<ui-root>` has loaded the dropdown. That works: every
			component "upgrades" a property set before its definition (it deletes the shadowing own property
			and re-sets it through the setter).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Frameworks">
		<markdown>
			The contract holds in React 19, Vue 3.5, Svelte 5, Solid, Angular 20 and Preact 10. One line each:

			| Framework | Usage                                                                                                                 |
			| --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
			| React 19  | `<ui-dropdown options={arr} value="x" open onui-changed={fn} />`                                                       |
			| Vue 3.5   | `isCustomElement: (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-")`, then `<ui-dropdown .options="arr" value="x" open @ui-changed="fn">` |
			| Svelte 5  | `<ui-dropdown options={arr} value="x" open onui-changed={fn}>`                                                         |
			| Solid     | `<ui-dropdown options={arr} value="x" open={o} on-ui-changed={fn}>`                                                    |
			| Angular   | `[options]="arr" (ui-changed)="fn($event)"` plus `CUSTOM_ELEMENTS_SCHEMA`                                              |

			- Define the elements before the framework renders them. As a backstop, a property set before
			  definition is upgraded (above).
			- Vue may send `open="false"` when it can't find a property; the boolean converter reads that as
			  false.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Styling">
		<markdown>
			Components use shadow DOM, so page CSS doesn't reach inside. Instead:

			- **tokens** (`--ui-font-size`, `--ui-primary` ...) inherit into every component;
			- **utilities** (`ui-stack`, `ui-bold` ...) work on your markup and inside components;
			- **`::part()`**: each component names its inner boxes (`ui-button::part(button)`);
			- **the app stylesheet**: give one stylesheet `id="ui-app-stylesheet"` and every component adopts
			  it, so it can restyle component internals with Fomantic's class grammar.

			See [Theming](/theming) for the details, and each component's Theming tab for its own tokens.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```css
			:root {
			  --ui-primary: oklch(55% 0.2 290);
			}
			ui-button.wide {
			  --ui-button-padding-inline: 3em;
			}
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Updating">
		<markdown>
			`npm update spell-ui` (or `yarn up spell-ui`) is all there is: no
			installer to rerun, no build to redo. Theme sheets and your token overrides are plain CSS, so they
			survive an update untouched. [`CHANGELOG.md`](https://github.com/spell-app/spell/blob/main/ui/CHANGELOG.md)
			lists what changed.
		</>
	</>
</spell-page>
